This has two implementations of mechanisms to resolve issue  #47 (see below):

NOTE1 we are far out of our sandbox here, when adding accessors to the c++ ABI library.  However, those accessors do not require new internal support - and are already exposed in libc++abi.  Nevertheless, more debate on the optimal ABI for contacts support is likely.

In the first implementation, we make use of an interface already exposed by libc++abi (and trivially implemented in libsupc++) - this allows us to store a pointer to the current exception into the violation object (in this version the reference count of the object is incremented, and therefore we no longer have a trivial dtor for the violation_object).

In the second version we take advantage of the fact that the exception object cannot be affected by other threads until there is an exception_ptr to refer to it - so that we capture the thrown object pointer (but do not increment the reference count *unless/until* the user actually calls `evaluation_exception()` as part of constructing the returned exception_pointer.  This means that, once again the violation_object has a trivial dtor.

NOTE2: this is a very brief description of the result of a bunch of analysis and checking of implementation divergence between libsupc++ and libc++abi - but it does exploit facilities which have common behaviour (and I'm actually hopeful that we could bring them into agreement and then upstream the agreed result to the Itanium ABI .. maybe I'm too optimistic ;) )

```c++
violation_handler (contract_violation& v)
{
  try {
   throw "foo";
 } catch () {
  exception_ptr p = v.evaluation_exception();
}
}
```
p is specified to point to the exception that was thrown iff the violation handler was called because of a throw in the evaluation of the condition. At present, we would return a pointer to the "foo" exception.

